                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-green-700">Typically 6+</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upfront Price</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-green-700">Lower</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-yellow-600">Higher</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our practical advice: for a bedroom, nursery, or home office where the fan will run for long stretches and noise matters, spend the extra money on a DC motor — you'll notice the difference every single day. For a garage, covered patio, or occasional-use guest room, a well-reviewed AC fan is a smarter place to save.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Running costs -->
        <div class="max-w-none">
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4">CFM Per Watt: The Spec That Actually Compares Two Fans</h3>
          <p>
            Airflow alone tells you how much air a fan moves. Wattage alone tells you what it costs. Neither is useful on its own, which is why the number worth calculating is airflow efficiency: CFM divided by watts. Divide a fan's high-speed CFM by its high-speed wattage and you get a single figure that lets you compare a 42-inch budget fan against a 70-inch great room fan on equal terms. Energy Star certification requires roughly 122 CFM per watt at high speed for a standard fan, and the best DC fans clear 200. A mediocre AC fan lands somewhere in the 60 to 80 range.
          </p>
          <p>
            Run the numbers on our picks and the gap is stark. The Haiku Luxe moves 6,306 CFM on under 30 watts, better than 210 CFM per watt. The Harbor Breeze Mazon moves considerably more raw air at 7,300 CFM, but it draws about 90 watts to do it, landing near 81 CFM per watt. Both are the right fan for their job — the Mazon is moving air across a 500-square-foot great room, which the Haiku cannot do — but the Haiku produces its airflow for roughly a third of the electricity per unit of air moved.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Ceiling Fan Actually Costs to Run</h3>
          <p>
            The math is straightforward. Multiply watts by hours used, divide by 1,000 to get kilowatt-hours, and multiply by your electricity rate. At a national-average rate of about 17 cents per kilowatt-hour and 8 hours of daily use, here is what that works out to across the fans in this guide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
