        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Scenarios -->
        <div id="scenarios" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Fan for Which Room
          </h2>
          <p>
            Rankings are useful, but nobody buys a ceiling fan in the abstract. They buy one for a specific room with a specific ceiling and a specific problem. Here is how our picks map onto the situations people actually shop for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Primary Bedroom</h3>
          <p>
            Noise is the deciding factor and nothing else comes close, because this is the one room where the fan runs while you are unconscious and unable to tolerate anything about it. Buy a DC motor. The six-speed granularity matters more here than anywhere else in the house: with three speeds you frequently find that speed one is not quite enough and speed two is too much, and you spend the summer splitting the difference badly. Prioritize a warm 2700K to 3000K light kit that dims smoothly to a low level, because a fan light that jumps from off to bright is useless for a middle-of-the-night trip across the room. The Modern Forms Wynd is the value answer here at 52 inches for a standard bedroom; the Haiku Luxe is the answer if the budget allows and the room runs hot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Nursery or Kid's Room</h3>
          <p>
            Same noise priority as the primary bedroom, with two additions. First, white-noise value: a fan on low is a genuinely effective, cheap sleep aid for infants, which argues for a fan whose lowest speed is stable and quiet rather than one that hunts and surges. Second, control placement — a fan a toddler can reach the chain on is a fan a toddler will play with, so remote or wall control is preferable to pull chains. The Westinghouse Comet at 42 inches suits most nursery-sized rooms and keeps the controls out of small hands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Basement or Converted Attic</h3>
          <p>
            Ceiling height decides this one before any other consideration. Under 8 feet you need a flush hugger mount, full stop, and the Hunter Dempsey exists specifically for that. Basements bring a second issue that fan shoppers underestimate: humidity. A basement that runs above 60 percent relative humidity will not be fixed by air movement alone, and a fan is a comfort improvement layered on top of proper moisture control, not a substitute for it. If your basement smells musty, deal with that first — our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best dehumidifier for a basement</a> covers sizing and drainage — then add the fan for circulation. Converted attics have the opposite problem, trapping heat at the peak, where a fan in reverse during winter and forward during summer earns its keep more than in any other room in the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Great Room or Open-Concept Space</h3>
          <p>
            The failure mode here is an undersized fan that produces a pleasant breeze directly beneath it and nothing at all 10 feet away, which is why the Harbor Breeze Mazon at 70 inches is on this list. Two secondary details matter in big rooms. Vaulted ceilings need a long downrod to bring the fan down out of the peak, because a fan mounted tight to a cathedral apex cannot pull air from above and effectively strangles itself. And in an open-concept space the fan light frequently has to serve as primary lighting for a zone, which pushes you toward a high-output kit rather than the 900-lumen accent kits common at this size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Kitchen</h3>
          <p>
            Kitchens are the room most often skipped and frequently should not be. The considerations are different: you want a neutral 3500K to 4000K light for food prep rather than a warm living-room glow, you want a blade finish that wipes clean because airborne cooking grease will settle on the leading edges, and you want to keep the fan well away from the range so it is not disrupting the hood's capture. A flush-mount fan positioned over the seating or island side of the room, not over the cooktop, is the arrangement that works.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Covered Porch or Open Patio</h3>
          <p>
            The entire decision comes down to the UL rating, covered in detail in the next section, and the mistake is nearly universal: people buy damp-rated fans for wet locations because damp-rated fans cost less and the porch has a roof. A roof is not the test. The test is whether wind-driven rain can reach the fan. Under a pergola, an open gazebo, or a patio cover with any gap, it can. The Minka-Aire Xtreme H2O is a genuine wet-rated fan and it is the one we would put anywhere weather actually reaches. Outdoor spaces also justify a larger blade span than the equivalent indoor square footage would suggest, because open air disperses the column faster than four walls do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rental or Flip</h3>
          <p>
            Different objective entirely: you want the lowest cost that will not generate a maintenance call. That argues for an AC fan with a socket-based light kit, because a tenant can replace a bulb and cannot replace an integrated LED module, and for dual mounting hardware so one SKU works across units with different ceiling heights. The Honeywell Belmar checks all three boxes, and its physical reverse switch means there is no remote to lose between tenancies.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-3">If You Only Remember One Thing</p>
            <p class="text-gray-800">
              Match the fan to the room's three fixed constraints in this order: ceiling height decides the mount type, square footage decides the blade span, and exposure decides the UL rating. Every one of those is a property of your house that you cannot change after the purchase. Motor type, finish, light color, and controls are preferences, and preferences can be traded off. Constraints cannot.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Installation -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installation &amp; Electrical Box Safety
          </h2>
          <p>
            This is the section we consider genuinely non-negotiable, because it's the one place where getting a ceiling fan installation wrong can be dangerous rather than just disappointing. A ceiling fan weighs significantly more than a standard light fixture — often 15-50 pounds before you factor in the vibration and dynamic load of spinning blades — and it needs to be mounted on hardware engineered for that job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Critical Safety Note</p>
            <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">
              Standard light-fixture electrical boxes are NOT rated to support a ceiling fan. A plain light box is typically rated for only a few pounds of static weight and has no rating at all for the constant vibration and dynamic load a spinning fan produces. Mounting a fan on one is a real safety hazard — the box can pull loose from the ceiling over time, and a fan falling from overhead is a serious injury risk. Before installing any ceiling fan, confirm your electrical box is specifically listed as fan-rated.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How to Tell If Your Box Is Fan-Rated</h3>
          <p>
            A fan-rated electrical box will have "acceptable for fan support" or similar UL-listed language stamped directly on the box itself, along with a maximum weight rating, commonly in the 35-70 pound range depending on the box. If you can't find this stamp, or if the box predates your home's most recent renovation and its rating is unknown, treat it as unrated and replace it before installing a fan. This applies even if a light fixture has hung from that exact box for years without issue — supporting a static light fixture and supporting a vibrating, spinning fan are entirely different structural demands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mounting to a Joist vs. a Fan-Rated Brace</h3>
          <p>
            The strongest mounting method is a fan-rated electrical box lag-screwed directly to a ceiling joist. When the fan location doesn't line up with a joist, the alternative is a fan-rated adjustable brace bar that spans between two joists and locks in place, with the fan-rated box attached to the brace itself. These brace bars are widely available and designed exactly for this situation — never substitute a standard remodel box or a light-duty pancake box in place of one, regardless of how sturdy it looks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step-by-Step Installation Overview</h3>
          <p>
            Turn off power at the breaker and confirm with a non-contact voltage tester before touching any wiring. Remove the existing fixture and box if it isn't fan-rated, and install a fan-rated box or brace bar per the manufacturer's instructions. Attach the fan's mounting bracket to the box, then wire the fan following the included diagram, matching black to black, white to white, and green or bare copper to the ground screw. Hang the fan motor housing on the mounting bracket hook, which frees both hands for the final wiring connections. Attach the blades, then the light kit, and finally the wall control or remote receiver if the fan uses one. Restore power and test all speeds, the light dimmer, and the reverse switch before considering the install complete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">When to Call an Electrician</h3>
          <p>
            Hire a licensed electrician if your home's wiring is older knob-and-tube or aluminum wiring, if you're adding a fan where no fixture previously existed and new wiring or a new circuit is needed, or if you're not fully confident identifying and replacing an unrated electrical box. Fan installation itself is a manageable weekend project for most homeowners, but the electrical box and wiring work underneath it is exactly the kind of detail that's expensive to get wrong and cheap to get inspected.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Smart Fans and Neutral Wire Requirements</h3>
          <p>
            One installation detail that trips up shoppers upgrading to a smart ceiling fan with light: many app-connected fans and their wall controls need a neutral wire present at the switch box to power the receiver, in addition to the standard hot and ground. Most homes built after the mid-1980s have a neutral wire bundled into every switch box, but older homes sometimes don't. Before ordering a smart fan, open the existing wall switch and confirm a neutral wire is present, or budget for an electrician to run one. Skipping this check is a common reason smart ceiling fan installs stall out mid-project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Wet vs Damp vs Dry -->
        <div id="outdoor-ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Wet vs. Damp vs. Dry Rated: What Outdoor Fans Actually Need
          </h2>
          <p>
            If you're shopping for an outdoor ceiling fan with light for a porch, patio, or gazebo, the UL rating stamped on the box matters more than the finish or the price tag. Installing the wrong rating for your specific space is the single biggest reason outdoor fans fail early, warping, rusting, or shorting out well before an indoor-rated fan ever would in the same time frame.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rating</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where It's Meant For</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Handles</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dry-Rated</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Indoor rooms only</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">No humidity or moisture exposure of any kind</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Damp-Rated</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Enclosed or fully covered porches</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Humidity and condensation, no direct rain</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet-Rated</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Open patios, gazebos, pergolas</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Direct rain, sun exposure, and standing moisture</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The distinction that trips up most shoppers is between damp-rated and wet-rated. A damp-rated fan is engineered to resist humidity and occasional condensation, which makes it suitable for a fully enclosed, roofed porch that never actually gets rained on directly. It is not built to withstand direct water contact — the motor housing and light kit seals aren't designed for it, and repeated rain exposure will cause premature corrosion and electrical failure. A wet-rated fan, by contrast, is built from the ground up for direct exposure: sealed motor housings, corrosion-resistant hardware, and weatherproof blade materials like ABS composite that won't warp or delaminate the way painted wood-composite blades do under repeated wet-dry cycles.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule: if wind-driven rain can reach the fan at any point — under a pergola, an open gazebo, or a patio cover with gaps — you need a wet-rated fan, not a damp-rated one. If your porch is fully enclosed with solid walls on at least two sides and a complete roof, a damp-rated fan is adequate and typically less expensive. When in doubt, spend the extra money on the wet rating; replacing a corroded damp-rated fan after two rainy seasons costs more in the long run than buying the correct fan the first time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 10: Noise, wobble, maintenance -->
        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Noise, Wobble, and Long-Term Maintenance
          </h2>
          <p>
            Most ceiling fan complaints are not manufacturing defects. They are installation details that were slightly off on day one and got worse over a few hundred hours of vibration. The good news is that nearly all of them are fixable in under an hour with tools you already own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Fan Noise Should Sound Like</h3>
          <p>
            Some sound is unavoidable — you are moving several thousand cubic feet of air a minute. The question is what kind of sound. Here is what we measured across our test fans and what each range means in practice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Measured Level</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Comparable To</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 35 dB</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A quiet library</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-green-700">Excellent, sleep-friendly at any speed</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">35-45 dB</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A refrigerator hum</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-green-700">Fine for bedrooms as background noise</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">45-55 dB</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quiet conversation</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-yellow-600">Acceptable in living spaces, not bedrooms</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 55 dB</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal speech</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-red-700">Something is wrong, or the fan is poorly built</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Diagnose by the character of the sound. A steady low hum that rises with speed is motor noise, normal on AC fans and the main thing DC motors eliminate. A rhythmic whoosh or thump that pulses once per blade pass is blade wash, usually from blades set too close to the ceiling or a downrod that is too short. A high-pitched electrical whine that is present with the light on and gone with it off is the LED driver, not the motor. And a rattle or click is loose hardware, which is the one to chase down immediately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixing a Wobble, in Order</h3>
          <p>
            Work through these in sequence and stop when the wobble goes away. Skipping to the balancing kit first is the most common mistake, because a balancing weight applied to a fan with a loose bracket just hides a problem that keeps getting worse.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>First, kill the power and check every screw.</strong> Tighten the blade bracket screws where each bracket meets the motor housing, the screws where each blade attaches to its bracket, the downrod set screws, and the screws holding the mounting bracket to the electrical box. Vibration loosens all of these over the first few months, and roughly half the wobbles we have chased were solved right here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Second, check that the blades are coplanar.</strong> Hold a yardstick vertically against the ceiling so its edge just touches the tip of one blade, then rotate the fan by hand and see whether each remaining blade reaches the same point. A blade sitting a quarter inch off from the others is a bent blade iron. You can often bend it gently back; if it is a stamped steel iron that has taken a set, replacing that single iron is cheap and worth it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Third, check for a warped blade.</strong> Lay all blades on a flat floor and stack them. Any blade that does not sit flush with the rest is warped, which happens most often with wood or MDF blades in humid rooms. Warped blades need replacement as a matched set, not individually, because mixing an old and a new blade reintroduces the imbalance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fourth, and only now, use a balancing kit.</strong> Clip the provided plastic weight to the middle of one blade, run the fan, and note whether the wobble improved or worsened. Work around the blades until you find the one where the clip helps most, then slide the clip along that blade to find the best position, and finally stick the adhesive weight to the top of the blade at that spot. It is tedious and it works.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing a balancing kit cannot fix: a fan that wobbles because the electrical box itself moves. Push up gently on the canopy with the power off. If the whole assembly shifts, the problem is the box or the brace bar, not the fan, and that is a safety issue rather than an annoyance. Go back to the installation section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Twice-Yearly Maintenance Routine</h3>
          <p>
            Ceiling fans ask for very little, and the little they ask for prevents nearly every long-term complaint. Twice a year — a good habit is to pair it with the seasonal direction change — do the following with the power off at the breaker.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dust the blades using a pillowcase slipped over each blade and pulled back toward you, which traps the dust rather than dropping it on the floor and, more importantly, keeps it off the leading edge. Dust buildup on blades is not just cosmetic: an uneven dust layer is itself a source of imbalance and wobble, and a heavy coating measurably reduces airflow. Wipe the motor housing and, on outdoor fans, check for the first signs of corrosion around fasteners. Re-tighten the blade bracket and downrod screws, which takes two minutes and heads off the wobble described above. Clean the light kit lens or glass, because a hazy diffuser can cut usable output noticeably. Finally, flip the direction switch for the coming season, and while you are there confirm it actually reverses — a reverse switch that has not been touched in three years sometimes sticks halfway, leaving the motor in a stalled state.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not oil the motor unless the manufacturer specifically calls for it. Nearly every fan sold in the last two decades uses sealed, permanently lubricated bearings, and adding oil to a sealed bearing does nothing useful while attracting dust to any surface it reaches. A handful of older Hunter models with genuine oil-bath motors are the exception, and those have a clearly marked fill port.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When the Remote Stops Working</h3>
          <p>
            Before assuming the receiver has failed, work through the cheap causes. Replace the remote battery, which is the answer more often than anything else. Cut power at the breaker for a full 30 seconds and restore it, which forces most receivers to re-enter pairing mode. Then re-pair by holding the remote's pairing button within 30 seconds of power-up, per your manual. If the fan has DIP switches in both the remote's battery compartment and the receiver, confirm the switch positions match exactly — they ship matched but get bumped during installation surprisingly often.
          </p>
          <p>
            If none of that works, the receiver is likely dead. Universal fan receiver kits are inexpensive and fit most fans, and replacing one is a 30-minute job involving dropping the canopy and swapping four wire connections. That is very often a better outcome than replacing a fan that is otherwise fine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Long a Ceiling Fan Should Last</h3>
          <p>
            A decently built fan installed correctly should run 10 to 20 years, and the motor is rarely what fails. What fails first, in order, is the pull chain switch on budget fans, the remote receiver on remote-controlled fans, and the LED driver on integrated light kits. All three are replaceable parts. Before you replace a whole fan because the light stopped working or the remote went dead, price the individual component — it is frequently under 30 dollars, and the motor that is still perfectly good has another decade in it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #1</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Installing on a Standard Light Box</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Reusing the light fixture box that was already in the ceiling is the most dangerous shortcut in ceiling fan installation. Standard boxes aren't rated for fan weight or vibration and can fail over time. Always confirm the box is UL-listed as fan-rated before mounting anything with spinning blades to it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #2</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Undersizing the Blade Span</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">A 42-inch fan in a 300-square-foot living room will run constantly and still feel like nothing is happening. Measure your room's square footage first and use the sizing table above — the fan needs to match the room, not the other way around.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #3</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Ignoring Reverse Mode in Winter</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Most fans include a reverse switch that most owners never touch. Running the fan clockwise at low speed in winter pulls warm air pooled near the ceiling back down along the walls, which can measurably improve heating comfort and reduce how hard your furnace has to work.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #4</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Choosing a Fan Too Loud for a Bedroom</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">A fan that sounds fine in a showroom or a busy living room can be genuinely disruptive at 2 a.m. in a quiet bedroom. If the fan will run overnight, prioritize a DC motor and check for published decibel or noise ratings before buying rather than after.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #5</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Expecting the Light Kit to Light the Room</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Most integrated fan light kits produce 800 to 1,800 lumens, and a 200-square-foot bedroom needs closer to 4,000. Treat the fan light as ambient lighting alongside lamps or recessed cans, and if it genuinely has to be the only fixture, shop specifically for a high-output kit.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #6</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying Damp-Rated for a Wet Location</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">A roof over the patio is not the test — whether wind-driven rain can reach the fan is. Damp-rated fans installed under pergolas and open covers corrode and delaminate within a season or two. The wet-rated fan costs more once; the damp-rated one costs less every two years.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #7</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Wiring a Light Dimmer to the Fan Motor</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Standard dimmers chop the AC waveform in a way an inductive motor cannot tolerate, producing hum, heat, and eventual failure. Fan speed needs a fan-rated speed control or the fan's own receiver. Use a combination fan-and-light control if both share one switch leg.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #8</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Leaving the Fan Running in an Empty Room</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">A fan cools people, not rooms — the effect is evaporative wind chill on skin, and an empty room gets nothing from it but a little waste heat off the motor. The savings a ceiling fan delivers come from raising the thermostat while you are in the room, not from running it around the clock.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What size ceiling fan do I need for my room?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For rooms up to 75 square feet, use a 29-36 inch blade span. Rooms up to 175 square feet suit 42-48 inch fans. Rooms up to 350 square feet need 52-56 inch fans. Great rooms up to 500 square feet require 60-70 inch fans for effective air movement. When between two sizes, size up rather than down — a slightly larger fan run at a lower speed is quieter and more comfortable than a smaller fan straining at full speed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a DC motor ceiling fan worth the extra cost?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For bedrooms, home offices, or anyone sensitive to noise, yes. DC motors run significantly quieter than AC motors and use roughly a third of the electricity at comparable speeds, with more speed settings for finer control. For utility spaces like garages where noise and efficiency matter less, a standard AC motor fan is a reasonable way to save money.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a ceiling fan on a standard light fixture box?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">No. Standard light fixture electrical boxes are not rated to support the weight and vibration of a ceiling fan and can fail, causing the fan to fall. You need a fan-rated electrical box, listed for at least 35-70 pounds of static and dynamic load depending on the fan, mounted to a joist or a fan-rated brace bar. This is a genuine safety issue, not a formality.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between damp-rated and wet-rated outdoor ceiling fans?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Damp-rated fans are built for covered, sheltered locations that experience humidity but not direct rain, such as an enclosed porch. Wet-rated fans are built to handle direct rain and sun exposure and are the correct choice for open patios, gazebos, or any space where the fan could get rained on. Using a damp-rated fan in a wet location leads to premature corrosion and electrical failure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my ceiling fan wobble?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Wobble is most often caused by unbalanced blades, a loose blade bracket screw, or an improperly secured mounting bracket. Use a blade balancing kit to correct minor wobble, and check that all bracket and downrod screws are torqued to the manufacturer's specification before assuming the fan itself is defective. A wobble that appears suddenly after years of smooth operation often points to a loosened mounting bracket rather than the blades themselves.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should ceiling fans spin clockwise or counterclockwise?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">In summer, fans should spin counterclockwise (when viewed from below) to push cool air straight down and create a wind-chill effect. In winter, reverse the fan to spin clockwise at low speed, which pulls cool air up and pushes warm air trapped near the ceiling back down along the walls, improving heating efficiency and comfort without raising the thermostat.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How many lumens should a ceiling fan light kit produce?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">If the fan is the only fixture in the room, aim for roughly 20 lumens per square foot in a living room or bedroom, so a 200-square-foot bedroom wants about 4,000 lumens total. Most integrated ceiling fan LED light kits produce between 800 and 1,800 lumens, which means a fan alone rarely lights a room adequately unless you supplement it with lamps or recessed cans. Shop by lumens rather than watts — with LEDs the two no longer track each other.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my ceiling fan light flicker when dimmed?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Flicker at low dim levels almost always means the dimmer and the LED driver are mismatched. Older leading-edge (TRIAC) dimmers were designed for 60-watt incandescent loads and cannot hold a stable waveform at the low wattage an LED draws. Replace it with a trailing-edge or LED-rated dimmer whose minimum load your light kit exceeds. If the fan came with its own dimming remote or wall control, use that and leave the wall switch as plain on-off rather than stacking two dimming circuits.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should my ceiling fan downrod be?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Blades should hang 8 to 9 feet above the floor for the best mix of airflow and safety. On a standard 8-foot ceiling use a flush or hugger mount. On a 9-foot ceiling use a 6-inch downrod, 10-foot a 12-inch rod, 11-foot an 18-inch rod, and 12-foot a 24-inch rod — roughly 6 inches of rod for every additional foot of ceiling. You also want at least 10 inches between the blades and the ceiling and 18 inches between the blade tips and the nearest wall.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do more blades mean better airflow?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">No. Blade count affects sound and appearance far more than airflow. What actually determines air movement is blade pitch, motor torque, and blade span. A well-engineered three-blade fan frequently outperforms a five-blade fan of the same diameter, because each added blade also adds drag the motor has to overcome. More blades do tend to produce a smoother, less pulsing sound, which is a real benefit in a bedroom — just not an airflow one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a ceiling fan?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A typical AC ceiling fan draws 60 to 90 watts on high. At 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, running it 8 hours a day costs roughly $2.50 to $3.75 a month. A DC fan drawing under 30 watts costs about $1.20 a month for the same use. Either is trivial next to central air conditioning, and the real saving comes from thermostat headroom — most people stay comfortable about 4 degrees warmer with a fan running, and each degree cuts cooling energy by 3 to 5 percent.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a ceiling fan on a sloped or vaulted ceiling?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, but you need a downrod and a mounting ball or canopy rated for the slope. Most standard canopies handle up to a 15 to 20 degree pitch; steeper cathedral ceilings need a dedicated sloped-ceiling adapter. The downrod must also be long enough to keep blade tips at least 18 inches from the nearest wall or ceiling surface, and you should measure clearance on the low side of the slope rather than at the peak.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are smart ceiling fans worth it?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">They are worth it if you will actually use scheduling, temperature automation, or voice control, and if your switch box has a neutral wire. Living with one, the feature that changed our habits was temperature automation rather than voice control. If you mainly want to avoid getting out of bed, a simple remote-control fan delivers most of the benefit for far less money and without depending on a manufacturer's app staying online for the fan's 15-year service life.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

